WEBVTT

s1.1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.783
Symphony reads its tickets from a tracker, and it posts notices to a chat.

s1.2
00:00:04.783 --> 00:00:08.072
The tracker is Linear, or GitHub Issues on a Projects board.

s1.3
00:00:08.072 --> 00:00:11.056
One key in WORKFLOW.md picks it: tracker.kind.

s1.4
00:00:11.056 --> 00:00:14.207
The notices go to Slack, to Discord, or to both.

s2.1
00:00:14.557 --> 00:00:17.331
With Linear, the states are the team's workflow states.

s2.2
00:00:17.331 --> 00:00:20.520
With GitHub, they are the options of the board's Status field.

s2.3
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.309
Todo, In Progress and Rework are active. The implementer works there.

s2.4
00:00:24.309 --> 00:00:27.945
In Review and Merging are parked. There the merger owns the ticket.

s2.5
00:00:27.945 --> 00:00:32.481
Done, Canceled and Duplicate are terminal. On GitHub, so is a closed issue.

s2.6
00:00:32.481 --> 00:00:39.622
Canceled and Duplicate are abandon states. A ticket moved there closes its open questions and its pull requests.

s3.1
00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:43.207
A Linear ticket keeps its key, like ENG-42.

s3.2
00:00:43.207 --> 00:00:47.911
A GitHub ticket is the repository name and the issue number, like app-42.

s3.3
00:00:47.911 --> 00:00:52.213
A ticket needs every required label, such as symphony, to be dispatched.

s3.4
00:00:52.213 --> 00:00:57.550
A repo label names the repository. On GitHub, the default is the issue's own.

s4.1
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.914
An agent can split a large ticket into sub-issues, in one tool call.

s4.2
00:01:01.914 --> 00:01:04.587
On Linear, their order becomes blocks relations.

s4.3
00:01:04.587 --> 00:01:09.278
On GitHub, they go on the board in the first active state, with blocked-by dependencies.

s4.4
00:01:09.278 --> 00:01:14.611
Only these links hold a ticket back, until every blocker is terminal. A parent link does not.

s4.5
00:01:14.611 --> 00:01:18.597
The tool is idempotent. A second call makes no second issue.

s5.1
00:01:18.947 --> 00:01:23.543
The agent keeps one comment on the ticket, headed Workpad, and edits it in place.

s5.2
00:01:23.543 --> 00:01:27.207
It writes through linear_graphql or github_graphql, run on the host.

s5.3
00:01:27.207 --> 00:01:32.514
On Linear, evidence is an uploaded file of up to 25 MB, attached to the issue.

s5.4
00:01:32.514 --> 00:01:38.757
GitHub has no API to upload a file to an issue. So evidence is text, in a collapsible comment.

s5.5
00:01:38.757 --> 00:01:45.597
That comment holds at most 65,536 characters. A screenshot is refused.

s6.1
00:01:45.947 --> 00:01:50.090
When Symphony moves a GitHub ticket to a terminal state, it closes the issue.

s6.2
00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.889
Done closes it as completed, the others as not planned. Any other state reopens it.

s6.3
00:01:54.889 --> 00:01:58.656
The pull request description starts with Closes and the issue reference.

s6.4
00:01:58.656 --> 00:02:04.169
When the train merges, GitHub closes the issue, and the board's Item closed workflow sets Done.

s6.5
00:02:04.169 --> 00:02:08.103
With Linear, the Linear and GitHub integration moves the ticket to Done.

s6.6
00:02:08.103 --> 00:02:13.360
The merger finds the ticket by the branch, a reference, a closing keyword, or the title.

s7.1
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:19.122
Twelve events go to the chat, from a question for a person to a failure under investigation.

s7.2
00:02:19.122 --> 00:02:21.357
Each configured channel gets each event.

s7.3
00:02:21.357 --> 00:02:27.824
Slack takes a bot token and a channel, or an incoming webhook. Discord takes a bot, or a channel webhook.

s7.4
00:02:27.824 --> 00:02:30.214
When both methods are set, the bot wins.

s7.5
00:02:30.214 --> 00:02:34.550
With no channel, nothing is sent. And a failed notice never fails a run.

s7.6
00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:39.475
A notice about a ticket also reaches its assignee, as a direct message from the bot.

s7.7
00:02:39.475 --> 00:02:46.579
A question in Slack names the ticket and who decides, with one button per option. Its direct message has the same buttons.

s7.8
00:02:46.579 --> 00:02:53.030
notify.direct_messages turns direct messages off, and notify.direct_events picks the events.

s8.1
00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:58.160
notify.users maps a GitHub login to a Slack member, and to a Discord user.

s8.2
00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.951
When a pull request goes back to octocat, the notice mentions octocat's mapped user.

s8.3
00:03:02.951 --> 00:03:05.298
A login with no mapping gets no mention.

s8.4
00:03:05.298 --> 00:03:10.765
On Discord, allowed mentions let only that user be pinged. Never a role, everyone, or here.

s8.5
00:03:10.765 --> 00:03:15.753
A Discord message holds at most 2,000 characters. The text is cut first.

s9.1
00:03:16.103 --> 00:03:20.417
The API takes webhooks on two routes: one for Linear, one for GitHub.

s9.2
00:03:20.417 --> 00:03:23.739
Each checks an HMAC signature over the raw body.

s9.3
00:03:23.739 --> 00:03:29.444
Ticket events wake the daemon. Pull request and CI events wake the merger, for that repository.

s9.4
00:03:29.444 --> 00:03:32.986
With the GitHub tracker, issue and board events wake the daemon too.

s9.5
00:03:32.986 --> 00:03:38.720
A webhook is only a hint. The loop reads the source again, so a lost delivery costs only time.

s9.6
00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.434
Setup makes both secrets and URLs, and shows the secrets only once.

s10.1
00:03:43.784 --> 00:03:47.247
The merger's main watch reads the finished CI runs on main.

s10.2
00:03:47.247 --> 00:03:51.452
When main turns red, it opens one ticket with the logs of the failed jobs.

s10.3
00:03:51.452 --> 00:03:56.715
On Linear, it goes on the board's team or project. On GitHub, it is an issue on the board.

s10.4
00:03:56.715 --> 00:04:00.432
Its routing labels make the daemon dispatch an agent to fix main.

s10.5
00:04:00.432 --> 00:04:05.236
When main is green again, the watch comments, moves the ticket to Done, and sends a notice.

s10.6
00:04:05.236 --> 00:04:12.242
In the mocked runs, the ticket opened in 2.3 seconds on Linear, and in 2.7 seconds on GitHub.
